### FEATURE_AI_INSIGHT — what the history says, and what a person decides about it
###
### Every request here is a real one against a running instance. Sign in first: the session cookie and
### the cross-site token are what the chain expects, and a request without them answers 401 for a
### reason that has nothing to do with the endpoint being right.
###
### Ports: the demo backend runs on 8081 (Tomcat9 owns 8080 on this machine).
###
### **No model is involved in any request in this file.** Every finding below is arithmetic over the
### product's own history — a median, a count, a date subtraction — computed locally, reproducible, and
### checkable by hand against a scoped export. That is DECISION-AI-PROVIDER-BOUNDARY-01's claim made
### walkable: most of this product's analytical value needs no provider at all.
###
### One permission governs the reading: AI_INSIGHT_VIEW, held by the owner and by managers, and
### delegable. **It grants no authority to change anything.** Applying a finding requires whatever
### permission the subject's own feature demands for that edit, checked by that feature — so signing in
### as somebody who holds the view and not the edit is the only honest way to see that the refusal is
### real. The last section does exactly that.

@host = http://localhost:8081
@owner = maria@atelier.ro
@employee = andrei@atelier.ro
@password = a-long-enough-passphrase

### Copy identifiers out of the responses into these, and the rest of the file works.
@processTemplateId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@taskTemplateId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### Sign in as somebody who may read findings and act on them.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{owner}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###############################################################################
### The reading — AI-INSIGHT-MISSING-STEP-01, SLOW-STEP-01, BLOCK-PATTERN-01,
### FALSE-DEPENDENCY-01
###############################################################################

### Everything the runs of one process template say about it.
###
### An empty list is an answer and the commonest one: below its floor a finding does not exist, and the
### product says nothing rather than hedging. A hedge is read as a recommendation by anybody in a hurry,
### which is everybody — so a template with two completed runs answers `[]` and that is correct.
###
### Each finding carries `findingKey` and `action`. The key is what the two routes below name; `action`
### is null on SLOW_STEP and BLOCK_PATTERN, where there is nothing for the product to apply and the card
### therefore renders no Apply at all.
GET {{host}}/api/insights?subject_type=process_template&subject_id={{processTemplateId}}

###############################################################################
### AI-INSIGHT-UNUSED-TEMPLATE-01 — the library's own finding
###############################################################################

### What the library says about one task template.
###
### Reported only for an approved template, only once ninety days have passed since it was approved, and
### only if nothing has been stamped from it inside that window. A template approved last week and never
### used is not stale — it is new, and saying otherwise a fortnight after somebody wrote it is how a
### curation queue loses its contributors.
###
### `daysSinceLastUse` is -1 where there never was a use. Never used and long unused are different
### findings and the card says different words.
GET {{host}}/api/insights?subject_type=task_template&subject_id={{taskTemplateId}}

### 400 — a subject this feature cannot reason about. An empty list would read as *there is nothing to
### say about it*, which is a claim about the subject rather than about the request, and a caller with a
### typo would spend an afternoon believing it.
GET {{host}}/api/insights?subject_type=biscuit&subject_id={{taskTemplateId}}

### 400 — the subject type is required. Not a 500: the server did not break, the request was short of
### something the caller can add.
GET {{host}}/api/insights?subject_id={{taskTemplateId}}

###############################################################################
### AI-INSIGHT-ACT-ON-INSIGHT-01 — Apply
###############################################################################

### Make the change the finding proposes. 204.
###
### **The request names a finding and never describes a change.** The product recomputes what that
### finding proposes and does that — so there is no field here in which a caller could ask for a step of
### their choosing at a position of their choosing, and two mutating ports sit safely behind a permission
### that only grants reading.
###
### The change goes through the subject feature's own edit path: TASKLIB retires (it never deletes, so
### the two hundred tasks stamped from a template keep their provenance), PROCESS inserts a step at the
### position the runs kept adding it, or removes an edge with its cycle validation applying as ever.
POST {{host}}/api/insights/apply
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjectType": "task_template",
  "subjectId": "{{taskTemplateId}}",
  "kind": "UNUSED_TEMPLATE",
  "findingKey": "unused"
}

### 409 — that finding is no longer current. Run the same request twice and the second answers this.
###
### Two situations arrive here and they are one: somebody edited the subject, or somebody else pressed
### the same button first. Both mean the product no longer computes the finding, and the surface's
### correct response is to recompute and show what is true now. Not a 404 — nothing is missing.
POST {{host}}/api/insights/apply
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjectType": "task_template",
  "subjectId": "{{taskTemplateId}}",
  "kind": "UNUSED_TEMPLATE",
  "findingKey": "unused"
}

### 400 — a sort of finding this build does not produce. `UNKNOWN_INSIGHT_KIND`, pointing at `kind`.
###
### Its own code rather than the subject type's: a caller whose subject type is perfectly good and whose
### kind is a typo would otherwise be sent to check the one field in their request that was not wrong.
POST {{host}}/api/insights/dismiss
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjectType": "task_template",
  "subjectId": "{{taskTemplateId}}",
  "kind": "BISCUIT",
  "findingKey": "unused"
}

### 400 — that finding is informational and proposes no change.
###
### Unreachable from the product's own screens, where Apply is absent rather than disabled on these
### kinds, and refused here anyway: a guarantee that holds only while every client behaves is not a
### guarantee. The fix for a slow step is a judgement about how the business works, and inventing one at
### the endpoint would be the product acting on a conclusion it never drew.
POST {{host}}/api/insights/apply
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjectType": "process_template",
  "subjectId": "{{processTemplateId}}",
  "kind": "SLOW_STEP",
  "findingKey": "client sign off"
}

###############################################################################
### AI-INSIGHT-ACT-ON-INSIGHT-01 — Dismiss
###############################################################################

### Put a finding away for good. 204, and nothing anywhere changes except the record of the judgement.
###
### **Permanent.** Not next month, not with a larger sample, not *you dismissed this but the evidence is
### now stronger*. The person made a judgement the system cannot see — they know the sequence is a client
### expectation, or the template is for work that comes round each January. Resurfacing it says *we think
### you were wrong*, which the system has no grounds for, and one resurfaced dismissal teaches a person
### that dismissing does not work.
###
### The single exception: the subject is re-authored. A dismissal about a process is a judgement about
### the process somebody had, and editing its steps or its edges makes it a judgement about something
### they no longer have. More completed runs of an unchanged template do **not** lift it — re-read the
### finding above after a few more runs and it stays absent.
POST {{host}}/api/insights/dismiss
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjectType": "process_template",
  "subjectId": "{{processTemplateId}}",
  "kind": "SLOW_STEP",
  "findingKey": "client sign off"
}

### Dismiss renders and works on every kind, including the two that cannot be applied. A finding nobody
### can act on and nobody can silence is a finding that nags, and a layer that nags is one the reader
### stops reading — permanently, and not as a bug.

###############################################################################
### The refusal that matters — UC-09 extension 2a
###############################################################################

### Sign in as somebody who may read findings and may not edit the subject.
###
### There is no role with that pair, and there is a **person**: AI_INSIGHT_VIEW is delegable and
### TASK_TEMPLATE_RETIRE is not, so an employee covering for a manager on holiday holds exactly one of
### the two. Lend the view first — through the delegation surface, or with
### `insert into auth_user_permission (user_id, permission_name) select id, 'AI_INSIGHT_VIEW' from
### auth_user where email = 'andrei@atelier.ro';` against the demo database.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{employee}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 200 — he may read the finding. That is what was lent to him.
GET {{host}}/api/insights?subject_type=task_template&subject_id={{taskTemplateId}}

### 403 — and it is TASKLIB refusing, not this feature.
###
### Seeing that something is wrong and being allowed to change it are different rights. This feature
### grants no authority and re-implements no authorization: it asks, and the feature that owns the
### subject answers. Deleting the check on TASKLIB's published surface turns this into a 204, which is
### how it was proven rather than assumed.
POST {{host}}/api/insights/apply
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjectType": "task_template",
  "subjectId": "{{taskTemplateId}}",
  "kind": "UNUSED_TEMPLATE",
  "findingKey": "unused"
}

### 204 — Dismiss still works for him. Putting a suggestion away is not an act of authority over
### anybody's library, and withholding it would leave him with advice he can neither take nor silence.
POST {{host}}/api/insights/dismiss
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjectType": "task_template",
  "subjectId": "{{taskTemplateId}}",
  "kind": "UNUSED_TEMPLATE",
  "findingKey": "unused"
}

###############################################################################
### Slice E — the three deterministic corrections
###############################################################################

### The estimate correction, with the clause that stops it being applied blindly
### (AI-INSIGHT-ESTIMATE-DIVERGENCE-01, DECISION-SAMPLE-DISCLOSURE-01).
###
### Read the evidence before the claim. Where `excludedRuns` is present, the template has stamped work
### that never closed, and the median below is the median of what survived — which is not a random
### sample of what was attempted. A template whose difficult half stopped closing and one whose work is
### genuinely fast return the same `medianWorkMs`; only `excludedRuns` separates them.
###
### `excludedRuns` is **absent, never zero**, where nothing material was excluded. A response carrying
### `"excludedRuns": 0` would be a defect: the card renders the second clause on presence alone, so a
### zero would put "0 further tasks never closed" on every card in the product.
GET {{host}}/api/insights?subject_type=task_template&subject_id={{taskTemplateId}}

### 204 — Apply writes the observed median through TASKLIB's own edit path, with TASKLIB's own
### permission (TASK_TEMPLATE_CREATE, the one its edit endpoint demands). Tasks already stamped keep
### what they were told: a template edit reaches future stamps and future runs, never a run in flight.
POST {{host}}/api/insights/apply
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjectType": "task_template",
  "subjectId": "{{taskTemplateId}}",
  "kind": "ESTIMATE_DIVERGENCE",
  "findingKey": "estimate"
}

### The cadence-aware idleness window (AI-INSIGHT-UNUSED-TEMPLATE-01, DECISION-CADENCE-WINDOW-01).
###
### On an UNUSED_TEMPLATE finding, `expectedIntervalDays` says which rule produced it. Present means a
### rhythm was found in the template's own usage and the template has missed an expected occurrence;
### absent means no rhythm was detectable and the workspace's configured window applied, exactly as it
### did before the cadence rule existed.
###
### The case worth walking by hand: a template used at roughly 90-day intervals and last used 80 days
### ago returns **nothing at all** for this kind. It is on schedule, not idle, and the fixed window
### would have proposed retiring it.
GET {{host}}/api/insights?subject_type=task_template&subject_id={{seasonalTemplateId}}

### The two thresholds both of the above are judged by (WORKSPACE-CONFIGURE-01, V60).
###
### Owner-only and not delegable. Neither number is a constant anywhere in the insight layer: the
### coverage share decides when a finding states what it could not see, and the idle window is the
### fallback for templates with no detectable rhythm. Read fresh on every computation, never cached —
### a held value is indistinguishable from a correct one until somebody changes the setting.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/settings
